# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed entirely |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Also removed |
| `ThreadAbortException` | Never thrown by runtime |
| Manual thread management | Use `CancellationToken` + `Task` or `BackgroundService` |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;

public class BackgroundProcessor : BackgroundService
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _forceCts = new();

    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        // Link the host's token with our force-stop token
        using var linkedCts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(
            stoppingToken, _forceCts.Token);
        var token = linkedCts.Token;

        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token); // pass token to unblock waits
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Called by the host during shutdown. Gives the loop time to exit gracefully,
    /// then forces cancellation if it doesn't.
    /// </summary>
    public override async Task StopAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        // Base class signals the stoppingToken passed to ExecuteAsync
        await base.StopAsync(cancellationToken);

        // If still running after host's shutdown timeout, force it
        if (!cancellationToken.IsCancellationRequested)
            return;

        _forceCts.Cancel();
    }

    public override void Dispose()
    {
        _forceCts.Dispose();
        base.Dispose();
    }

    // Stub methods — replace with real implementations
    private Task<object> GetNextItemAsync(CancellationToken ct) => Task.FromResult<object>(null!);
    private Task ProcessItemAsync(object item, CancellationToken ct) => Task.CompletedTask;
    private void RollbackCurrentTransaction() { }
    private void FlushBuffers() { }
}
```

**Registration in `Program.cs`:**
```csharp
builder.Services.AddHostedService<BackgroundProcessor>();
```

## Common Pitfalls

1. **Blocking calls ignore cancellation** — If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a `BlockingCollection` or socket, cancellation won't interrupt it. Always use the async/cancellable overload (e.g., `Channel<T>.ReadAsync(token)`).

2. **`Thread.Sleep` → `await Task.Delay(timeout, token)`** — Sleep can't be cancelled; `Task.Delay` respects the token.

3. **Don't swallow all `OperationCanceledException`** — Only catch it `when` the token is actually cancelled, otherwise you hide bugs.

4. **Shutdown timeout** — The default host shutdown timeout is 30 seconds (`HostOptions.ShutdownTimeout`). Configure it if your cleanup is slow:
   ```csharp
   builder.Services.Configure<HostOptions>(o => o.ShutdownTimeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));
   ```

5. **If you must keep raw threads** (e.g., COM/STA affinity), replace Abort with cooperative cancellation:
   ```csharp
   // Instead of _workerThread.Abort():
   _cts.Cancel();
   if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)))
       Environment.FailFast("Worker hung during shutdown");
   ```

6. **`volatile bool` is fragile** — It can't unblock a waiting call. `CancellationToken` both signals *and* wakes async waits.
